  "description": "Method replication with the paper's own panoramic photos of the same places (the original showed 360-degree videos of them): participants give age and gender, then visit 15 urban green spaces in the West Lake scenic area of Hangzhou in random order as horizontal panoramic photos (scroll sideways, at least 15 s each). For each place they rate valence and arousal on the 9-point Self-Assessment Manikin, rate five perceived plant attributes (vegetation layers, tree shape, tree texture, colour diversity, cultural connotation; 1-5), and tick which of six factors would make them use the place; at the end they say how often they visit such green spaces. Rebuilt from Qiao, Zhuang, Zhang, Su & Xia (2021), International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 18(16): 8526, with the panoramas printed in the paper's Figure A1, the manikin figures and wording of its Questionnaire 2, and the items of its Table A2 and Questionnaire 1 (CC BY 4.0). Analysis as in the paper: mean SAM valence and arousal per place; Pearson correlations and backward linear regression of valence and arousal on the plant attributes, use-function score and the places' measured structure (size, edge shape index, canopy density, water and plant share, listed in the paper's Table 2); ANOVA across structure levels; t-tests by gender. Differences from the original study: (1) the original showed one-minute 360-degree panoramic videos; here participants view the paper's horizontal panoramic photos (1269 x 288 px from the published appendix); (2) no facial-expression recording (the paper also estimated valence and arousal from webcam video with a face-recognition model); (3) the paper's GAPED baseline pictures are not included (not openly licensed); (4) ratings come right after each place instead of in one questionnaire at the end; (5) the plant attributes are phrased as statements ('The layers of vegetation are abundant') where the paper's table asks 'Whether the layers of vegetation are abundant'; (6) age and gender questions and the per-place minimum viewing time are our additions.",
  "logoPosition": "right",
  "completedHtml": "<h3>Thank you for completing the survey.</h3>",
